※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `std.text` and `std.⍕` are the text of a value, in one definition for every type.
※
※ One definition and not one per type: the language has no overloading, so a glyph has one
※ meaning per number of operands, and a generic body with `comptime if` is what says a
※ different thing for each type inside that one meaning.  An arm not taken is not checked,
※ which is what lets the arms differ in shape.
※
※ **Nothing in the number arm names a width.**  The type it is compiled for settles what the
※ arithmetic is, so one body is every integer type the language has.  And nothing is ever
※ negated: the remainder of a negative number is negative and its digit is what that is away
※ from nought, which is the only way to write the smallest value a type has -- there being no
※ positive of it.
※
※ **What comes out reads back as what went in.**  The raised minus is the one a negative
※ literal is written with, and a record is written in the notation a program writes one in.

let std := ⎕import("std")

type Point = x: u8 ; y: i16
type Line = from: Point ; to: Point

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    if ⍕0u8 ≠ "0":
        return 1u6
    if ⍕1234u32 ≠ "1234":
        return 2u6
    ※ The smallest value a signed type has, which has no positive.
    if ⍕⁻128i8 ≠ "⁻128":
        return 3u6
    if ⍕⁻1i64 ≠ "⁻1":
        return 4u6
    if ⍕true ≠ "true" ∨ ⍕false ≠ "false":
        return 5u6
    if ⍕"already text" ≠ "already text":
        return 6u6
    ※ A record this module was never told about, written by reflection over its fields.
    let l: Line = Line(.from ← Point(.x ← 1u8, .y ← ⁻234i16),
                       .to ← Point(.x ← 56u8, .y ← 7i16))
    let want: str = "Line(.from ← Point(.x ← 1, .y ← ⁻234), .to ← Point(.x ← 56, .y ← 7))"
    if ⍕l ≠ want:
        return 7u6
    ※ And the same thing under its name, which is what a program delegates to: it
    ※ takes the arena, `⍕` with no arena being the one that names `⎕heap` for you.
    let scratch: mut arena = ⎕arena
    if std.text(42u16, &mut scratch) ≠ "42":
        return 8u6
    ⎕empty(scratch)
    0u6
